  <h1 class="part">Part 1 — Foundations</h1>

  <h2>1.1 Aapka pehla tween</h2>
  <p>Ek <strong>tween</strong> ek single animation instance hota hai - ye kisi target ki ek ya zyada properties ko time ke saath change karta hai.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.to(".box", {
  x: 300,        // 300px right move karo
  rotation: 360, // ek full turn spin karo
  duration: 2,   // 2 seconds mein
  ease: "power2.out"
});</code></pre>

  <h2>1.2 Char core tween methods</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Method</th><th>Kya karta hai</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gsap.to()</code></td><td>Current values → given values tak animate karta hai</td><td><code>gsap.to(".box", { x: 300 })</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gsap.from()</code></td><td>Given values → current values tak animate karta hai (entrance animations ke liye best)</td><td><code>gsap.from(".box", { opacity: 0, y: 50 })</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gsap.fromTo()</code></td><td>Aap khud start aur end dono values set karte ho</td><td><code>gsap.fromTo(".box", {opacity:0}, {opacity:1, duration:1})</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gsap.set()</code></td><td>Instantly values set karta hai, koi animation nahi</td><td><code>gsap.set(".box", { opacity: 0 })</code></td></tr>
  </table>

  <pre><code class="language-javascript">// from() "reveal" animations ke liye workhorse hai —
// ye given state SE animate karke element ki natural/current
// CSS state tak wapas leke aata hai.
gsap.from(".card", {
  y: 60,
  opacity: 0,
  duration: 0.8,
  ease: "power3.out"
});</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">⚠️ <strong>Common pitfall:</strong> <code>from()</code> ke saath, GSAP starting values ko <strong>turant</strong> apply kar deta hai, jisse "from" state ka flash dikh sakta hai agar element pehle se visible tha. Isko <code>autoAlpha</code> (opacity + visibility) ke saath combine karo.</div>

  <h2>1.3 Duration, delay, aur ease</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.to(".box", {
  x: 200,
  duration: 1.5,   // seconds
  delay: 0.3,      // shuru hone se pehle 0.3s wait karo
  ease: "elastic.out(1, 0.4)"
});</code></pre>

  <h2>1.4 Kuch bhi tween karna - CSS, transforms, SVG, colors, plain objects</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">// CSS transforms (GPU accelerated — hamesha top/left se better)
gsap.to(".box", { x: 100, y: 50, scale: 1.5, rotation: 45 });

// Regular CSS properties
gsap.to(".box", { backgroundColor: "#ff6b6b", borderRadius: "50%" });

// SVG attributes
gsap.to("circle", { attr: { r: 80, fill: "#5227FF" } });

// Plain JavaScript objects (sirf DOM elements nahi!)
const counter = { value: 0 };
gsap.to(counter, {
  value: 100,
  duration: 2,
  onUpdate: () => console.log(Math.round(counter.value))
});

// Scroll position
gsap.to(window, { scrollTo: { y: 500 }, duration: 1 });</code></pre>

  <p>Ye last example beginners ke liye key insight hai: <strong>GSAP sirf DOM elements hi animate nahi karta.</strong> Ye <em>kisi bhi JavaScript object ki properties</em> ko animate karta hai.</p>

  <h2>1.5 Relative values aur special string syntax</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.to(".box", { x: "+=100" });   // current position se 100px aur right
gsap.to(".box", { x: "-=50" });    // 50px left move karo
gsap.to(".box", { rotation: "+=360" }); // ek aur full turn spin karo
gsap.to(".box", { scale: "*=2" }); // current scale ko 2 se multiply karo
gsap.to(".box", { x: "random(-100, 100)" }); // har baar random value</code></pre>

  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.to(".box", { width: "50%" });
gsap.to(".box", { x: "10rem" });</code></pre>

