  <hr>
  <h1 class="part">Part 3 — Scroll-Driven Animation (ScrollTrigger)</h1>

  <p>ScrollTrigger woh plugin hai jiske liye GSAP sabse zyada jaana jaata hai. Ye kisi bhi tween ya timeline ki playback ko user ke scroll position se link karta hai. Ye 2025 se free hai aur <strong>kisi bhi</strong> scroll container ke saath kaam karta hai.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">import { ScrollTrigger } from "gsap/ScrollTrigger";
gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);</code></pre>

  <h2>3.1 Fundamentals: trigger, start, end, markers</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.to(".box", {
  x: 400,
  scrollTrigger: {
    trigger: ".box",   // element jo animation trigger karta hai
    start: "top 80%",  // jab .box ka TOP viewport mein 80% neeche pahunche
    end: "top 30%",    // jab .box ka TOP viewport mein 30% neeche pahunche
    markers: true       // 🔴 sirf dev ke liye visual debug markers
  }
});</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">⚠️ Hamesha <code>markers: true</code> ke saath develop karo — production ke liye ise hata dena.</div>

  <h2>3.2 <code>scrub</code> aur <code>pin</code> — do sabse important properties</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.to(".box", {
  x: 500,
  scrollTrigger: {
    trigger: ".section",
    start: "top top",
    end: "+=1000",     // ye active rehte hue 1000px scroll karo
    scrub: true          // instantly scroll position se synced
    // scrub: 1          // YA: smoother feel ke liye 1 second ka "catch-up" lag
  }
});</code></pre>

  <pre><code class="language-javascript">ScrollTrigger.create({
  trigger: ".panel",
  start: "top top",
  end: "+=100%",   // ek extra viewport-height scroll ke liye pinned raho
  pin: true
});</code></pre>

  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.timeline({
  scrollTrigger: {
    trigger: ".panel",
    start: "top top",
    end: "+=1500",
    pin: true,
    scrub: 1
  }
})
  .from(".panel h2", { opacity: 0, y: 60 })
  .from(".panel img", { opacity: 0, scale: 0.8 }, "-=0.3");</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">⚠️ GSAP pinned element ko ek <code>.pin-spacer</code> <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> mein wrap kar deta hai. Pinning ke baad CSS toote toh is wrapper ko check karo — ye expected behavior hai, bug nahi.</div>

  <h2>3.3 Timelines + ScrollTrigger</h2>
  <p>Koi bhi GSAP timeline apne constructor mein directly <code>scrollTrigger</code> config le sakti hai — pehle poori story ko normal timeline ki tarah banao, phir end mein scroll control attach karo.</p>

  <h2>3.4 <code>toggleActions</code> aur scroll callbacks</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">gsap.from(".card", {
  opacity: 0,
  y: 40,
  scrollTrigger: {
    trigger: ".card",
    start: "top 85%",
    toggleActions: "play none none reverse"
    // = enter par play, leave par kuch nahi,
    //   enter-back par kuch nahi, leave-back par reverse
  }
});</code></pre>

  <pre><code class="language-javascript">ScrollTrigger.create({
  trigger: ".section",
  start: "top center",
  onEnter: () => console.log("entered"),
  onLeave: () => console.log("left, scrolling down"),
  onEnterBack: () => console.log("re-entered, scrolling up"),
  onLeaveBack: () => console.log("left, scrolling up"),
  once: true // sirf pehli baar fire, phir khud-b-khud kill
});</code></pre>

  <h2>3.5 Lists ko efficiently animate karne ke liye <code>batch()</code></h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">ScrollTrigger.batch(".list-item", {
  start: "top 85%",
  onEnter: batch => gsap.from(batch, { opacity: 0, y: 30, stagger: 0.15, overwrite: true })
});</code></pre>

